o make your telomeres longer  because while it might protect you from  cardiovascular disease it may increase  your risk for cancer quite dramatically  so this is like I was saying earlier you  can apply the same kind of approach to  lots of different phenotypes for a  specific disease and when you do that  for lung cancer you find as you should  that smoking is the most important  factor but also turns out biting maybe  off is also a risk factor so having high  vitamin b12 this is bad we don't really  know why and this preliminary result has  been confirmed now in a paper that I  think just is coming out in JAMA  oncology so I'm gonna close this pretty  soon there's a pretty complicated study  that was recently published doing the  same kind of analysis with the  metabolites so they did a metabolite  and they looked at lots of snips and  connected that to lung cancer when they  do that they find arachidonic acid  metabolite being very very strongly  associated with with lung cancer risks  so that that's sort of allowing us to  screen through lots of new sort of  phenotypes that might be associated with  lung cancer some older studies so  bilirubin has been associated with  protection from lung cancer and there's  a for example per surfactant protein B  is also a fairly robust biomarker for  the development of lung cancer I did  want to just man  in this truly massive study that was  done it was really designed to look I  think for cardiovascular disease and  bilirubin but um they studied about five  hundred thousand people and what they  found so these are people with normal  ranges of bilirubin but just kind of  varies and so there is a gel Barre  syndrome as an example where people are  born with a mutation in utt 1a1  influences their B to metabolize  bilirubin and so when their so that's  when they're when they're very young  they have to be sort of treated for that  but anyway there's lots of variability  in the population it turns out that that  higher biliru